BT 1 0 0 1 0 181 Tm /F1 11 Tf 16 TL 1.114256 Tw (Ethical human values \227 such as honesty, trust, respect, care, and justice \227 are widely) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.470549 Tw (acknowledged as essential for a fulfilling personal life and a harmonious society. Yet) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.547971 Tw (knowledge of these values alone does not guarantee that a person will actually live by) Tj T* 0 Tw .588239 Tw (them. A student may be taught, in theory, that honesty is important, and may even agree) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.856584 Tw (with the idea, but still act dishonestly under pressure or temptation. This gap between) Tj T* 0 Tw .939042 Tw (knowing a value and living it points to the central importance of motivation. Motivation is) Tj T* 0 Tw .065328 Tw (the internal driving force that moves a person from mere intellectual acceptance of a value) Tj T* 0 Tw .229299 Tw (to its consistent practice in thought, behaviour, and work. This assignment examines what) Tj T* 0 Tw .025256 Tw (motivation means in the context of Universal Human Values \(UHV\), why it is necessary for) Tj T* 0 Tw .398661 Tw (the genuine adoption of ethical values, the sources from which such motivation can arise,) Tj T* 0 Tw 5.041459 Tw (and the practical implications of this understanding for engineering students and) Tj T* 0 Tw (professionals.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 78 323.8898 cm
q
.101961 .101961 .101961 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 3 Tm /F2 13 Tf 16 TL (2. Understanding Motivation in the Context of Human Values) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 78 221.8898 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 85 Tm /F1 11 Tf 16 TL 2.327892 Tw (In ordinary usage, motivation refers to the reason or driving force behind a particular) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.920145 Tw (action. In the framework of Universal Human Values, motivation is understood more) Tj T* 0 Tw .240542 Tw (specifically as the inner conviction that arises when a person verifies a value through their) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.460756 Tw (own self-exploration and finds it to be true to their own natural acceptance, rather than) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.725542 Tw (something imposed from outside as a rule, reward, or fear of punishment. There is an) Tj T* 0 Tw (important distinction here between two kinds of motivation:) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 78 197.8898 cm
q
.101961 .101961 .101961 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 2.5 Tm /F2 11.5 Tf 14 TL (2.1 External or Imposed Motivation) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 78 97.88976 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 85 Tm /F1 11 Tf 16 TL .261584 Tw (This form of motivation comes from outside the individual \227 for example, following ethical) Tj T* 0 Tw .851828 Tw (conduct because of fear of law, fear of social disapproval, desire for reward, or pressure) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.974145 Tw (from parents, teachers, or an organisation. While such motivation can produce correct) Tj T* 0 Tw .896599 Tw (behaviour for a period of time, it tends to be unstable. As soon as external monitoring is) Tj T* 0 Tw .491738 Tw (removed, the behaviour is likely to change, because the person never actually owned the) Tj T* 0 Tw (value; they only complied with it.) Tj T* ET
